# Without this, a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows
# default) writes CRLF into the working tree while the committed content is LF.
# The scripts that parse these files are written against LF, so the pre-push
# audit fails on files that are perfectly valid, and the push is refused.
# Pinning the checkout to LF keeps the working tree byte-identical to what is
# committed, on every platform.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
